web-monitor local Debian package
===============================

Build on Debian (intended initial target: trixie or later):

    sudo apt install build-essential debhelper dh-python python3-all
    dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b
    sudo apt install ../web-monitor_0.5.0_all.deb

Runtime dependencies are resolved by apt. No pip install or virtual environment
creation happens during package installation. Debian trixie supplies aiortc
1.11; the prototype was developed and transport-tested with aiortc 1.15. The
1.11 minimum uses the same API, but that dependency combination needs a live
compatibility test on the target. Older releases may need dependency backports.

The package installs:

    /usr/local/bin/web-monitor                 launcher using /usr/bin/python3
    /usr/local/lib/web-monitor/                private Python modules
    /usr/local/share/web-monitor/static/       browser assets
    /etc/radio/web-monitor.json                dpkg-managed conffile
    /usr/lib/systemd/system/web-monitor.service
    /usr/share/doc/web-monitor/                documentation

The static/ symlink beside server.py preserves the source checkout's layout.
The code, assets, and configuration require no daemon write permission.
Systemd loads configuration through LoadCredential into a private read-only file.

This administrator-requested /usr/local layout is a deliberate Debian Policy
9.1.2 exception. debian/rules overrides dh_usrlocal; Lintian will flag the
layout. Do not submit this layout to the Debian archive unchanged. The unit and
Debian documentation still use their ordinary package-managed system locations.

Configuration is localhost-only with automatic _opus._udp.local. discovery
enabled. Choose discovery.interfaces to restrict browsing to your receiver LAN,
or use explicit groups from config.manual.example.json. Existing configuration
files retain their settings; set discovery to true to opt into discovery when
using a nonempty manual groups list. dpkg preserves local edits
on upgrade; package installation must not silently replace an existing config.

Before enabling the packaged unit, remove or relocate any old LOCAL unit at
/etc/systemd/system/web-monitor.service: it takes precedence over the packaged
unit. Inspect it first and retain intended local changes in a drop-in. An old
local unit may still invoke a .venv instead of the packaged system interpreter.

Then:

    sudo systemctl daemon-reload
    sudo systemd-analyze verify /usr/lib/systemd/system/web-monitor.service
    sudo systemctl reset-failed web-monitor
    sudo systemctl enable --now web-monitor

The package does not enable or start the service on install. Its upgrade scripts
may stop an already running service; restart it manually after upgrading:

    sudo systemctl restart web-monitor

Configuration under /etc is retained on removal and removed on purge, according
to normal dpkg conffile semantics. The shared /etc/radio directory is not purged
recursively. No maintainer scripts delete your radio configuration directory.

The current maintainer identity and copyright/license metadata are placeholders
for local development. Set the intended maintainer and license before release.
The full Debian build and systemd service need validation on Linux; the source
was initially developed on macOS. Tests require runtime dependencies and local
UDP/HTTP permission; run python3 -m unittest -v explicitly on the build/test host.

Frequency metadata also requires receiver-LAN input on UDP 5006 (same multicast
addresses as audio). No additional dependency or service permission is needed.

Source hostname resolution uses direct mDNS PTR queries on the gateway, not
NSS. It is enabled by default; source_names=false disables it.
